{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '205,285p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,100p' references_provenance.json; sed -n '1,100p' claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L924-L928]]\n\n\\subsection{Aggregate Comparisons and Completed Checks}\nThe equal-weight six-condition adaptive averages are coverage 0.8942 and volume 0.5446. Aggregate paired contrasts in Table~\\ref{tab:contrasts} favor adaptive ILR over the isotropic ILR reference in both coverage and volume, but not over Euclidean in volume. In particular, the Euclidean volume-reduction interval is entirely negative. This aggregate includes correlated C2-derived controls and the shifted condition, so it is not a population marginal coverage estimate or an estimate over six independent task families. The separate condition results, rather than this summary, establish the paper's central trade-off.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.marginal_coverage]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.normalized_volume]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrr}\n\\toprule\nComparator & Contrast & Mean & Paired bootstrap 95\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nEuclidean & Coverage: adaptive minus reference & $-0.0014$ & $[-0.0065,0.0039]$ \\\\\nEuclidean & Volume: reference minus adaptive & $-0.0346$ & $[-0.0546,-0.0115]$ \\\\\nILR ball & Coverage: adaptive minus reference & $0.0251$ & $[0.0185,0.0319]$ \\\\\nILR ball & Volume: reference minus adaptive & $0.0496$ & $[0.0306,0.0618]$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Equal-weight contrasts across all six conditions. Positive values favor adaptive ILR. Intervals resample five paired seed blocks and retain all conditions together; few-seed percentile intervals are unstable and not multiplicity-adjusted. They are not per-condition error bars.}\n\\label{tab:contrasts}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage_gap_vs_euclidean]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.volume_gain_vs_euclidean]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage_gap_vs_ilr]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.volume_gain_vs_ilr]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe final evaluation completed and satisfied its recorded qualification checks. Observed checks include query-order/subset invariance, singleton/full-simplex membership controls, matched interventions, and a replay. Their scope is operational: they do not establish conditional validity, shift robustness, a comprehensive nonadaptivity audit, or universal superiority. No unreported diagnostic is inferred from the qualification outcome. The performance failures above remain scientific limitations notwithstanding completion of these checks.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.final.qualification.status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.final.qualification.outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.final.qualification.correct]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\nThe information-removal controls help interpret compactness without establishing a mechanism. With constant covariates, a fitted region cannot distinguish test locations from their inputs, so a wide region reflects the heterogeneous response distribution left after removing that information. With deranged training pairs, the calibration/test association is unchanged but the fitted relationship is degraded. Independent calibration can still adjust a frozen score to the resulting marginal residual distribution. Neither control is equivalent to testing whether the intact model has recovered a particular conditional law. In particular, their near-nominal marginal coverage does not validate the intact model's local reliability. The useful contrast is the loss of compactness when predictive training information is removed, not a proof that every adaptive shape factor is necessary.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1007-L1036]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\nThe study separates two questions often collapsed into a single claim of reliable uncertainty. Independent calibration governs a marginal rank statement under a specified sampling law; geometry governs which compositions are included at a given calibrated threshold. An adaptive score can improve measured efficiency without improving every local coverage rate. Conversely, a large region can have poor coverage after a distribution change. These are different failures and require different evidence. Here the shifted law is documented, but attribution to a specific fitting component remains unresolved.\n\nThe common volume measure is useful precisely because it is coordinate-independent across the evaluated methods, not because it is the only appropriate utility. Uniform simplex volume puts weight on different locations than a boundary-concentrated response distribution. Consequently, near-unit volume does not imply near-unit response coverage. Application-specific costs, alternative base measures, or calibrated probability-forecast scores could change the practical ranking; they were not measured. The retained scalar objective should therefore not be interpreted as a proper probabilistic score or as a replacement for joint coverage and set size \\citep{Gneiting2007}.\n\nSeveral limitations constrain interpretation. The response families are synthetic, strictly positive, and low-dimensional relative to many applications. No real dataset, structural-zero case, label-set task, alternative score geometry, or weighted shift calibration was evaluated. The boundary and shifted conditions also change response family and dimension relative to the logistic-normal conditions; their comparison is a regime contrast, not an isolated intervention on boundary proximity or shift. The reference fits use fewer training rows than the adaptive location, and the auxiliary residual comparison bundles changes. Neither causal mechanism attribution nor an optimality claim follows.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L776-L814]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L665-L672]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]]\n\nOnly five held-out seed blocks are available, and their shared response/control structure limits the information in an aggregate interval. The frozen evaluation reduces direct reuse of development measurements; it is not a broad certification against all adaptive reuse. Exact reproduction also requires the stored resource arrays: their generator uses private entropy, so the published seed labels alone cannot recreate them. We provide readable evaluated source copies, resource manifests, complete retained numerical summaries, and deterministic figure/table construction. This supports inspection and replay using the distributed arrays, while explicitly preserving the limits of regeneration and of the evidence itself.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/final_resource_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L768-L814]]\n% @evidence[[derived/complete_results.json]]\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nTraining-adaptive ILR ellipsoids offer a favorable measured coverage--volume trade-off for the evaluated heteroskedastic logistic-normal responses, but that finding does not transfer uniformly across the simplex-valued conditions. The same configuration nearly fills the simplex for boundary-concentrated Dirichlet responses and remains below nominal coverage under covariate shift. Even the favorable condition retains pronounced sign-stratum imbalance. These limitations are part of the main result, not exceptions removed from the evaluation.\n\nThe methodological lesson is to compare geometries on the same response-space measure and to separate uncertainty sources. Calibration, finite-test coverage, integration precision, and seed-to-seed variability answer different questions. A study that reports only a pooled objective, a native-space radius, or a nominal coverage theorem can miss an unfavorable practical trade-off. The present evidence supports a narrowly scoped empirical comparison of complete fitted systems, with independent calibration and explicit failure analysis; it does not establish a uniquely effective component, conditional validity, real-data generalization, or robustness to unmeasured shifts.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\subsection{Exact Residual Geometry and Numerical Safeguards}\nThe inspected implementation is reproduced in \\path{source_evidence/adaptive.py}; the source inventory maps each copy to its evaluated location and SHA256. Features are formed at lines 78--85, training fits at lines 103--131, and row-wise factors at lines 190--198. The quadratic design has 21 columns: an intercept, five linear terms, five squares, and ten cross-products. With design matrix $\\Phi$, every ridge fit has the explicit form $(\\Phi^T\\Phi+\\lambda I)^{-1}\\Phi^TZ$, implemented through a linear solve. The location penalty is $0.01$; the log-residual-square penalty is $5$. The residual offset, variance-prediction clipping, and standardized-residual clipping are exactly those in equations~\\eqref{eq:scale}--\\eqref{eq:shape}. No test response enters these fits.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L78-L131]]\n\nThe contrast basis in \\path{source_evidence/evaluate_final.py}, lines 624--630, has column $j$ proportional to $(1,\\ldots,1,-j,0,\\ldots,0)$, normalized by $\\sqrt{j(j+1)}$, for $j=1,\\ldots,K-1$. Thus ILR coordinates span the sum-zero log-contrast space. Predicted boundary scores use the inverse-ILR location, not observed test responses. Both calibration and future queries use the same right-sided group routing. With fewer than four training rows, the implementation falls back to a full-data location, zero log-variance coefficients, and identity group factors; the released full-size evaluations do not exercise that branch. Empty training groups still receive the pooled positive shape through shrinkage. This operational fallback is distinct from the empty evaluation masks discussed below.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L624-L637]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L90-L131]]\n\nThe inverse transform maps a Euclidean ellipsoid in ILR space to a generally nonellipsoidal region in raw coordinates. The study consequently calls it an ILR ellipsoid and evaluates membership after the appropriate transform. Neither its ILR determinant nor a product of coordinate widths equals its raw-simplex volume fraction. Our volume comparisons use direct membership integration, avoiding a change-of-variables approximation.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L882-L938]]\n\n\\subsection{Synthetic Generators and Matched Interventions}\\label{sec:generators}\nEach stored resource has covariates $X_i\\sim N(0,I_5)$ before any test intervention. A seed-specific coefficient matrix has independent $N(0,0.35^2)$ entries, and the ILR mean is $\\mu(x)=x^TW+0.2x_1^2\\mathbf1$. For C1 and C2, the observed ILR response is $\\mu(x)+\\sigma(x)\\odot\\epsilon$ with independent standard-normal $\\epsilon$. C1 has constant coordinate scales $0.35$; C2 multiplies those scales by $\\exp\\{0.65\\clip(x_1,-3,3)\\}$ and a linear sequence from $0.5$ to $1.5$. These are data-generator parameters, not fitted hyperparameters.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L776-L795]]\n\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-07T06:32:13.622671+00:00\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"Direct identifier lookup: 10.1007/b106715\",\n    \"Direct identifie